    "unit_title": "Understanding and representing integers",
    "title": "Integers, opposites, and absolute value",
    "summary": "Identify integers, opposites, and absolute value.",
    "skill_codes": [
      "S1"
    ],
    "content_schema_version": 1,
    "content": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Integers, opposites, and absolute value"
        },
        {
          "type": "subheading",
          "text": "What is an integer?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "An integer is a whole number. It can be positive, negative, or zero. It has no fractional part and no decimal."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet_list",
          "items": [
            "-8, -3, 0, 5, and 12 are integers.",
            "3/4, 0.6, and 12% are not integers."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "We use negative numbers every day. A temperature 3 degrees below zero is -3. A debt of 500 pesos is -500. A diver 6 meters under the sea is at -6."
        },
        {
          "type": "subheading",
          "text": "Opposites and absolute value"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A number line shows integers in order. Zero is in the middle. Positive integers go right. Negative integers go left."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Opposites are two integers the same distance from zero on different sides. -5 and 5 are opposites."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Absolute value is the distance from zero. Distance is always positive or zero. We write it with two bars. |5| = 5, |-5| = 5, |0| = 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "image_asset": "lesson/lesson-1/opposites-example.png",
          "image_alt": "Number line showing -5 and 5 as opposites around zero"
        },
        {
          "type": "worked_example",
          "title": "Recognizing an integer",
          "problem": "Is -11 an integer? What is its opposite? What is |-11|?",
          "steps": [
            "Step 1: -11 has no fraction and no decimal. So it is an integer.",
            "Step 2: The opposite of -11 is 11. Same distance, other side of zero.",
            "Step 3: |-11| = 11 because -11 is 11 units from zero."
          ],
          "answer": "-11 is an integer. Opposite: 11. Absolute value: 11."
        },
        {
          "type": "common_mistake",
          "text": "|-5| = -5 is wrong. Absolute value is a distance from zero, so it is never negative. Correct: |-5| = 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "variant": "cta",
          "text": "Ready to practice. Continue to the regular exercise for 6 questions."
        }
      ],
      "source_text": "Lesson 1.1 - Integers, opposites, and absolute value\nSkill: S1\nObjective:\n1. Identify integers, opposites, and absolute value.\nWhat is an integer?\nAn integer is a whole number. It can be positive, negative, or zero. It has no fractional part and no decimal.\nExamples: -8, -3, 0, 5, 12.\nNot integers: 3/4, 0.6, 12%.\nWe use negative numbers every day. A temperature 3 degrees below zero is -3. A debt of 500 pesos is -500. A diver 6 meters under the sea is at -6.\nOpposites and absolute value\nA number line shows integers in order. Zero is in the middle. Positive integers go right. Negative integers go left.\nOpposites are two integers the same distance from zero on different sides. -5 and 5 are opposites.\nAbsolute value is the distance from zero. Distance is always positive or zero. We write it with two bars.\n|5| = 5\n|-5| = 5\n|0| = 0\nWorked example\nProblem: Is -11 an integer? What is its opposite? What is |-11|?\nStep 1: -11 has no fraction and no decimal. So it is an integer.\nStep 2: The opposite of -11 is 11. Same distance, other side of zero.\nStep 3: |-11| = 11 because -11 is 11 units from zero.\nAnswer: -11 is an integer. Opposite: 11. Absolute value: 11.\nCommon mistake\n|-5| = -5 is wrong. Absolute value is a distance from zero, so it is never negative. Correct: |-5| = 5."
    }
  },
